From c27bd19bb25f18cbe69755a1aa5e33320f231644 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HuskerMinion <28571744+HuskerMinion@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 35/40] clk: realtek: Add the RTD129x SCPU PLL driver

Expose the SCPU PLL as a clock so that cpufreq-dt can scale the CPU
frequency. The rate is 27 MHz * (NCODE + 3 + FCODE / 2048), divided by
1, 2 or 4 (CRT PLL_DIV scpu_freq_sel).

The sequence follows the vendor kernels (QNAP 4.2.8 clk-rtd129x.c,
Realtek BSP 4.9 clk-pll.c): NCODE/FCODE are written with OC_EN clear,
then OC_EN is set and the driver waits for OC_DONE, and 100 us more as
QNAP does. The divider is switched to or from /1 only while the PLL is
at 1 GHz (the vendor glitch workaround), and otherwise before or after
the PLL change so that the output never goes above the old or new rate.

Only the NCODE/FCODE pairs of the BSP frequency table are programmed,
and the output is limited to the vendor maximum of 1.4 GHz. If OC_EN is
clear at probe, the output rate is not known and the driver does not
register the clock. None of the vendor clock code takes the RTD129x
RBUS lock, so this driver does not either.
